sense of  darkness to that song that i love  it reminds me of sitting in my bedroom  right here where i  am now and just feeling a little bit  lost  feeling like i wasn't quite sure of my  direction and knowing that i wanted to  be a creative but being slightly unsure  as to how to get there and reminds me of  feeling a bit  confused but also hopeful and super  inspired my second song selection  is no surprises by radiohead radiohead  is one of my favorite bands of all time  and i discovered okay computer when i  was probably about  14 or so again there was something so  angsty to this record it's a record that  surrounds melancholy and alienation and  that first line you know a heart that  fills up like a landfill a job that  slowly kills you was like  like it was a hit and tom york's voice  was so  graceful and delicate and fragile almost  and the whole of radiohead's catalog  really taught me the idea of evolving as  an artist  it it kind of brings back that like  teenage angst when you're walking home  from school and you're kind of feeling  like no one understands you  and you're just wandering like lonely as  a cloud and feeling like  a little bit cool because you're  listening to this edgy band that no one  else in your class knows  i don't know that song feels soaring it  always lifted me up  next i've got figure eight by fk twigs  and the reason why i love  this song is that it's so kind of  confident in how  sensual and physical it is it's very  kind of muscular as a song and i  remember  listening to those synths and feeling  like it somehow got to the core of me  and it kind of reminds me of like  getting ready for a party  and then like putting it on with my  friends and we would like feel like we  were just in a movie  like we were just like getting ready  like we would walk in slow motion into  the party obviously we were like 15 and  awkward  um but that's the memory that comes back  to me when i think about that tune  what her music taught me is that idea of  experimentation  of fluidity also work ethic everything i  see on her social media she's always  honing her craft and her martial arts  and  her choreography are so ambitious and i  think it  kind of showed me that there are no  boundaries when it comes to music in the  worlds you can create  out getting ribs by king cruel is my  next election  and this album is probably one of the  most important albums when it comes to  who arlo parks is and  how she came to be there's something so  distinctly  london about this record it's poetic  it's gritty it's rough around the edges  and it really inspired me to start  making music  in my bedroom because i was listening to  his voice and it felt so unique and i  was listening to the instrumentation and  it was quite  sparse and minimal and it felt like he  had made it in his bedroom and that was  something that really drew me to his  music  and there's this little kind of sample  at the beginning and this guitar run and  it just reminds me  of walking home from school with a  friend of mine  and just feeling free i think what's  beautiful about those teenage years is  that there are those moments where you  feel  completely infinite and unstoppable and  you're like we're going to rule the  world someday and that's what outgetting  ribs reminds me  next up we've got yonkers by tyler the  creator  when i was younger i was an unabashed  odd future fan i loved how  provocative and playful and weird the  music was and i remember  this is one of the first raps that i  learned completely off by heart  and it's a very dark one um but  i remember that being something so  confident about his delivery  and the way the beat was kind of  creaking it was almost  gothic but it was also hip-hop and it  was this really interesting  fusion and i found it so exciting um and  tyler the creators definitely had a big  impact on my work  in terms of how versatile he is and the  places he explores with his production  up next juicy by biggie this song  ignited my love  of hop i think i heard this song when i  was pretty young  i think i must have been 30 in 12 or 13.  there was something so inspiring about  the storytelling  like i remember listening to it over and  over again  until i was just like wrapping it in the  kitchen and i told it to my little  brother  as well so even now whenever we hear  that song we just like  we know it word for word the story  behind that song you know  his ascent took the fame and building  his way up from the bottom i found it so  inspiring and the production  felt so nostalgic for a time that i  wasn't even  alive in it was a song that i grew to  when i was by myself and feeling a  little bit down  and his flow is literally like butter  the drums in hip-hop is something that i  really hold it to my heart how crunchy  they are  those kind of repeated loops you can  hear that influence in my music  so next up we've got special affair by  the internet  and i remember when i first discovered  this band i was in love with every  single member hearing sid's voice and it  was so it was like honey just like  floating towards me and i got obsessed  with that whole record ego death  i went to see them when i went to  reading festival i was 15  and i saw them live and i loved those  kind of psychedelic influences it felt  like i was dreaming or i was floating  even just seeing like these kind of  slightly indie edgy like black kids just  doing their thing  felt so inspiring to me as like a little  weird kid in the corner so  i was super inspired by watching them  play so next we've got  teenagers by my chemical romance i was a  big fan of my chemical romance when i  was a kid  especially like welcome to the black  parade and  this song was like the epitome of those  moments where you were just raging  against everything and i would just put  my headphones on my massive headphones  with my little like ipod nano and i  would stomp around i would like stop by  the river  and be like no one understands me it was  like the quintessential song  for like sulking essentially i had a big  phase where they were all i would listen  to them and fall out boy  and good charlotte as well so i was  definitely one of those kids because i  remember  loving like loving jared way with my  whole heart  like all of it and my last choice is  milk teas by keaton hanson  and this is a very kind of quiet soft  song  it encouraged me to kind of tap into  that vulnerable side of myself  and led to me discovering artists like  elliot smith and sophie and stevens  i think it reminds me of those moments  where i was just in my room  feeling a little bit frustrated and  keaton pence's voice has this very  soothing quality  it made me somehow feel like everything  was going to be all right so although  it's quite sad and delicate  i've got lovely memories of listening to  this and feeling like  things might work out in the end and  that's my ultimate playlist from my  teenage years  let me know what you thought in the  comments below and don't forget to check  out my newest album collapsed in  sunbeams  [Music]  you
 

